fix(pip): Transpose the lens projection matrix and measure it in the log

Hard numbers first: the lens texture and its source both average 11.84 of 255 while the main
view of the same frame averages 47.9. Identical values for the texture and its source prove the
publish and the post process are not the cause; the lens pass renders a much darker image.

That fits one mistake that explains all three symptoms at once. If the projection maps the whole
texture into the optic instead of the magnified patch, the lens shows a rectangular image (the
shader fades the sampled region out at the texture border), looks unmagnified (it is the whole
wide view) and is dark (a wide view of a dusk scene averages far lower than the optic's patch).

The candidate is the matrix convention: every engine shader consumes these matrices as row
vectors (mul(v, m), which is why the engine uploads them as they are), while the pack's lens
shaders write mul(scope_lense_reproject, float4(...)) - the column vector form. The matrix is now
transposed on upload so the pack's expression computes the mapping it was written for.

The publish diagnostic now also maps three main view NDC points through the same matrix and
prints the UVs they land on, so the next log states whether the lens samples a small centred
patch (centre near 0.5, the half offsets a small delta) or the whole texture (near 0 and 1)
instead of leaving it to judgement by eye.